From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Date: Wed, 02 Mar 94 20:40:42 GMT
Subject: [#94-3-38] Natural Rights

Tim Starr writes on rights:
>>From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
>>
>>If any "rights" existed in the Cambrian period, which rights existed?
>
>I don't know what the Cambrian period is, sorry.

This was about 510 to 600 million years ago, when marine life first became
abundant.  Rather primitive stuff -- e.g. trilobites and brachiopods.  I
was not sure whether any natural rights were supposed to have preceded
homo sapiens, but you later point out that there are no 'metaphysical
things external to people called "rights," ' and together with your two-
sentence proof from a few months ago on why beasts can't have rights
(which I like), this clearly answers my question about pre-human existence.

>Assuming it was a period
>of homo sapiens hunter-gatherer existence, I'd say that all possible
>negative rights and liberties (to freedom of action without interference
>from other people) existed and awaited our discovery and enforcement - those
>that weren't already, that is.

You mention "negative" rights so does this exclude the right to a free
data super-highway?  (It might be phrased as the right not to be charged
whilst using the data super-highway.)  It would be rather curious if the
right to a free data super-highway had an objective existence long before
the conception of a data highway.  On the other hand, if this right has
only existed for a short period it can't be quite so natural.

If the right to a free anything doesn't qualify as a possible negative
right or liberty, how about the right not to have your messages tampered
with on the data super-highway, or the right not to have your neighbour's
AI nanotech slaves run riot and invade your home?  In hunter-gatherer
times they hadn't even conceived of computers or nanotech but an infinity
of rights were supposed to have an objective existence!

>>>You're missing my point: I reject the artificial-natural dichotomy.
>>
>>A good suggestion.  We'll leave the Aristotelian bivalent logic, and bring
>>in a third category, spontaneous orders.  Allowing some overlap, we can
>>have some s.o.'s as obviously natural (e.g. biological evolution), and
>>some as "man-made" (e.g. money, posthuman evolution).
>
>I prefer the terminology of the Austrian school of economics, that s.o.'s
>are the results of human action but not human design.

That would describe the "man-made" s.o.'s such as money and memetic
evolution, but aren't there also natural s.o.'s such as biological
evolution and self-regulation of ecosystems?  (Those last two are
mentioned in the Extropian Principles, although if Austrian economics
doesn't need to say anything about natural s.o.'s we needn't assume it's
a point where Austrian economics and Extropianism split. :-)

>>>Ever seen a beaver dam?  Is it artificial or natural?  My answer is:
>>>both.
>> [...]
>I've got a borderline case to confuse you with: my grandparents built a
>foot bridge over a stream on our family's forest land in Upstate NY.  Then
>the beavers my grandfather had introduced to the land built a dam using
>the bridge as foundation.  Artificial or natural?

A good example of a spontaneous order.  I think this would have to go
right in the centre of my map -- a spontaneous order which was partly
the result of human action but not human design, and partly the result
of beaver action.  Doesn't seem fair to call it more natural or more
artificial.

>>Universally recognised enforcement could constitute evidence for the pre-
>>existence of rights.
>
>I'm afraid I don't see why this would be so, because I distinguish between
>the existence and enforcement of rights more strongly than you seem to.

If rights were never enforced or respected there would be no evidence for
existence or pre-existence; if enforcement or respect was totally
uniform (rather difficult) this would be good evidence for pre-existence,
and theories which allowed for natural rights would score highly in terms
of accurately predicting human action.  With things being somewhere
between these extremes, I'd prefer a theory which explained human action
in terms of goals.  

I'd say people develop their goals, values, hopes and fears, from which
behaviour could be computed quite accurately if you know the goals etc of
each individual.  Meanwhile, Schelling points evolve in interactions which
are found to be to the benefit of all parties, and some may term these
points "rights".  At other times individuals may consciously conceive of a
new specialised right that they think they should have, to help further
their goals.  Since rights can be spontaneous orders I now think it's
better to speak of "creation" rather than "construction".  Construction
implies design, creation may be with or without intent. 

The theory of rights seems to have an inherent inaccuracy, though, since
there is much uncertainty regarding enforcement and recognition.  Hardly
anyone even thinks about enforcing their right to non-coercion or right
to life, and statists certainly don't respect these.  If the theory
implies that rights will increasingly be enforced as they're discovered,
I hope it turns out to be true. 

>>If the answer to the question "specify all natural rights" was so obvious
>>that only an imbecile would get it wrong, I'd say this was reasonable
>>evidence that "natural" rights had an objective existence.
>
>It's the same as the answer to the question: specify all numbers.  Both
>are infinite, and thus impossible to comprehensively specify.

There seems to be uncertainty in the theory whether you have it that all
possible rights exist but they're not uniformly enforced, or that opinions
vary as to which rights are objective and which are fictions. 

>>I refer you to the answer I gave some moments ago.
>>[That's my "John Major in Parliament" impersonation.]
>
>Hey, I've watched the question period on CSPAN!  I know it's supposed to
>be: "I refer my right honorable friend to the answer I gave some moments
>ago."

The impersonation was admittedly a compromise, since I didn't want people
to get the impression that List members were becoming like politicians,
hating each other but hypocritically using terms like "Rt Hon."  Also,
substitute "reply" for "answer", omit "right" if addressee is not a Privy
Councillor, and at times substitute "the honorable gentleman".

