From: pgf@srl05.cacs.usl.edu (Phil G. Fraering)
Date: Wed, 2 Mar 1994 20:59:12 -0600
Subject: [#94-3-42] In Defense of Babylon 5

I'm a little suprised at the anti-Babylon-5 posts currently showing
up on extropians. I'd like to say a couple of words in defense of
it right now

Harvey Newstrom writes:

\...In the episode with the soul stealer the protagonist rescues souls from
/being stored in an interactive state, and releases them into the
\nothingness from which they came.  It was portrayed at the end of the
/episode as a beautiful moment as she crushed the globes one by one, killing
\each consciousness that had been stored after death.

It wasn't meant to be that clear. Only if you're absolutely sure of
the mechanistic view of conciousness (i.e. your religion is The
Extropian Institute ;-) is the episode that clear-cut. I could tell
long before I read jms's explanation that the whole *point* of the
episode is that the Minbari and the Soul Hunters have belief systems
where if one of them performs a good deed, i.e. storing souls, it is
perceived of as evil by the other (soul stealer!). And Delenn's
"liberation" of the souls _IS_, to the Soul Hunters, plain First
Degree Murder.

Disparate value systems are something that extropianism per se doesn't
seem to do well with, even if it's kinda what draws a lot of people to
libertarianism to begin with... questions like "what is human" will
still need to be addressed in the future, even if everyone's an
extropian, if there isn't to be a violent conflict of one sort or
another.

\In the episode with the biolgical technology, there were definite views
/against disturbing dead worlds (sacred burial grounds?) and trying to seek
\out "alien" technology.

There was a definite view against going over to a dead world, digging
up a piece of tech, and turning on the "on" switch, because you might
activate someone else's Gray Goo disaster. Actually, come to think of
it, didn't they have a Blue Goo disaster instead? ;-) (Hey, synchronicity
again, what with Trek's basement universe episode that I haven't seen
yet.)

\In the episode with the super-psychic Chekov, they seem to state that such
/increased mental powers are somehow off-limits to humans, and we have no
\right seeking such abilities.

No, just that such mental powers in the hands of a coersive state aren't
a very good idea. Or did you think the Psi Corps was some libertarian
fantasy being run out of Galt's Gulch by the folks from _Alongside Night_?

You know, cryptography's a good idea, let's all use clipper chips!

\In the episode where each culture shows its religion to the other cultures,
/they seemed to indicate that an exchange of religion, not science, would be
\the means of achieving peace in the galaxy.  At the end, the Earthman seems
/to "best" the other cultures by having hundreds of diverse religions to
\explore.

No, it was just another kooky idea someone thought up. And galactic
bloodshed is still a problem at the end of the episode. (BTW, "belief
system" != religion. Look at the Centauri celebration. I want to join
that belief system ;-) They haven't had time yet, but the only culture
(that is known about; noone knows the Vorlon religion. Maybe the Vorlons
don't "believe" but know and find G*d lacking ;-) ) that is totally 
uniform is the Minbari, because they burned all their heretics a while
back or something like that.

I *like* the fact that in the B-5 universe, humanity didn't suddenly
convert, wholesale, to atheism. I would think that unrealistic.

And hey, the first guy in the line was an atheist. That should tell
you something.

\I had hoped that Babylon 5 would be more realistic than Star Trek, as their
/portrayal of science seemed to indicate.  But now it seems that the limited
\scientific advancement is not due to scientific accuracy, but a belief that
/science is limited.  Note that the main characters seem to be psychics,
\security, and management types.  Few scientists are portrayed as positive
/models.

Look, just because everyone's not just like you, or just like the show's
creator (who is, just like Gene Roddenberry, an atheist), doesn't mean
it's not realistic. Maybe it means it is. Let's face it, humanity isn't
going to be homogenized anytime in the near future. If anything the new
technologies are going to make diversity a larger factor in the future.
Look at Nick's old post (if he still has it around and you have the thornes)
about the "enclave" cultures in the Valley that use the nets to link up
with their other enclaves around the world. And imagine what it would be
like if the net were more widespread. You might actually see a "virtual
nation" of all the Chinese merchants throughout SE Asia.

Anyway, I've ranted enough, I have to go eat and get some sleep.


