Not everyone accepts the larynx structures argument; e.g., Steven Pinker of
MIT (whose book _The Lagnuage Instinct_ just came out) believes that
even homo habilis (2 - 2.5 million years ago) could have had language.
As to the inheritance of words, that's a good point, and I find it
somewhat persuasive.  But what interests linguists is not words per se,
but grammar; i.e., the rules by which words are put together to express
ideas.  

Now, the interesting thing about grammar, the thing that Noam Chomsky
has made his career out of, is that there seem to be certain universal
(i.e., pan-linguistic) phenomena in it that you can't explain in terms of
functionality. Chomsky argues that such universals come from our genetic
endowment, as manifested in the "language organ" in our brain.  Other
linguists (e.g., UCLA's Ian Maddieson) have argued that some universals
that have been treated this way (e.g., some phonetic universals) may
just be remnants of the single proto-language spoken by our ancestors
before they split from Africa.  

Here's an analogy:  You land on a planet and find that every house there
is painted blue.  You know that there is some functional usage to houses,
but you can't figure out why blue is their only color, and nothing else
about the planet or the people there provides any clues.  So you are left
with two hypotheses: Either something about the brains of these people
causes them to choose blue paint for their houses (Chomsky), or they do
it because "that's the way it's always been done" (Maddieson).  Since
all the fossil evidence suggests that the house-building ability arose only
once in the prehistory of the planet, you can't use that evidence to 
distinguish between the two hypotheses.

Now, all of a sudden, someone finds some fossils suggesting that the
ancestors of the blue-house people split up into separate groups before
they had the ability to build houses; then, each of the sub-groups evolved
the ability independently.  Now, the only possible alternative is the
Chomsky version, since the favoring of blue could not have come from a common
ancestor.

I should admit that I see this debate as just another flavor of the
age-old nature/nurture chestnut.  The debate will go on and on, despite
evidence for either position, because of the overly simplistic, 
information-theoretic framework in which it is couched.  

Chomsky wanted to make linguistics like physics, but his models were Descartes 
and Newton. What is needed is a framework in which the red herring of 
nature/nurture  ceases to have any interpretation, a framework in which 
circular causality and chaotic behavior are the norm, rather than a cause for 
alarm.  In other words, we should be looking at late twentieth-century physical
scientists like Ilya Prigogine and Stuart Kauffman as models, and not clinging 
to the dualism and clockwork mechanical determinism that have been abandoned by
the rest of the scientific community.

-- Simon!

