From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Date: Thu, 03 Mar 94 18:34:11 GMT
Subject: [#94-3-48] PCR: The scarcity of objective certainties

There's been some discussion on the subject of certainty, and I support
the view that certainties are relative.  In other words, many instances
of "certainty" are really nothing of the sort due to epistemological
limitations standing between ourselves and reality.

True, there is a class of things which we may be certain about.  For
instance, "I think, therefore I am (something)".  I can be absolutely
certain about this, and I can be absolutely certain that I can be
absolutely certain about that....  So we've already got an infinite
regression of valid certainties.  Note that "I think" is only my
definition for something "I" experience, it doesn't mean that thinking is
necessarily what I think it is.  I might be running as a simulation on
some child's toy, although I consider it highly unlikely.  Also "I am"
doesn't allow me to deduce what I am, whether I'm on the hardware platform
I think I'm on, or I'm a spirit, or I'm on a simulator's computer.  What I
can be certain of is that some process is occurring which I term "my
thinking", and some thing exists in some world which I see as "myself".

The belief "I think, therefore I am" is a statement about objective
reality, but my certainty that I can be certain about this refers to
subjective reality.  So I'm only left with two objective certainties
"I think" and "I am".  All other "certainties" concerning objective
reality are almost certainly relative.  What about my belief that one
and one makes two?  I can remember previous times when I've performed the
calculation and found it to be true, and I can look at a pair of shoes and
"prove" it to myself.  But is this _absolute_ proof?  What if the laws of
mathematics are not what I think at all?  The simulators could have
tampered with my memories, and I might actually be insane, deliberately
programmed with a delusion of sanity.  And it would be much easier to hold
a false belief for something like 584,930 - 83,042.  The simulators would
merely have to modify my memory after I'd calculated the result, I needn't
even be insane.

Here's some examples of typical confidence values that I might assign to
various beliefs:-

* I think (whatever process "thinking" really is) [1]

* I think, therefore I am (something, possibly a simulation) [1]

* One and one makes two [1 - 10^-(10^200)]

* 584,930 - 83,042 = 501,888 [1 - 10^-22]

* I am not a simulation [1 - 10^-20]

* A multiple universe theory is true [0.99999]

* A consequence of many worlds is that individuals have _some_ control
over their journey through "reality-space", influencing the relative
subjective probabilities of experiencing alternate futures [0.8]

* A consequence of many worlds is that like events subjectively appear to
attract ("synchronicity") and unlike events repel, more than is
justified by a true random distribution [0.55]

* Socialism is a political system which raises the economic and
psychological wellbeing of the vast majority of people living under
its catchment areas [10^-15]

* Deathism is an extropic strategy which works to the benefit of the 
individual practising it [10^-20]

* Quarks are conscious [10^-23]

I can be certain about which beliefs I choose to hold, such as the belief
that entropic death-worshipping is a useless strategy.  But I can still
hold this belief open to criticism and give it a confidence value of, say,
[1 - 10^-20] rather than [1].  On the other hand, [1] is the confidence
value a Xtian would give to beliefs in mystical fiction.

"To be uncertain is to be unable to decide" is true if referring to
subjective reality, but I don't believe this applies to objective reality.
I can assign a confidence value [very close to one] to a belief, and
although I admit the belief is not certainly true, I can be certain that 
it's a better belief to hold than the alternatives.  Although I'm
uncertain as to the truth of the belief, that doesn't make me unable to
decide to believe it.  But anyone who was so mixed up in their beliefs
that they were unable to decide what their values and purposes were would
be in danger of losing their marbles.  

Steve Whitrow                           sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk

