Well, I think I need to learn more about praxeology, especially how it 
hooks up to fields of inquiry other than economics, but all I've got is 
HUMAN ACTION. (Aside to Tim Starr, I didn't mean to boast: "most" means 
"maybe half" in this case, really.)

> Eric again: <I don't know what I said that made you thinking I was advocating
> that we hold two opposites to be true simulatneously.>
> 
> When you hold that certainty is relative.

Okay, I think I see what you're getting at now. If I say that I am far 
more certain of the proposition that my bike exists than I am of the 
proposition that my bike does not exist, this seems to you as if I am 
saying both statements are true simultaneously? That's an odd way of 
looking at it.

Let me put it this way. I can say that I think it is highly likely that 
my bike exists, and I can say that it is highly unlikely that my bike does
not exist, and then I can say a third thing: that it is even less likely 
that *both* these propositions are true than that my bike does no exist. 
When you say that "belief that certainty is relative implies the ability 
to hold two opposites true simultaneously" you seem to claim that I am no 
more certain that the two opposites are not true simultaneously than I am 
certain that my bike exists. But the main point to my claim that 
certainty is relative is that I *am* in fact much more certain that two 
opposites are not simultaneously true than I am certain that my bike 
exists. Well, my bike is a bad example here, but I'm more certain of 
noncontradiction than I am of the existence of Deng Xiaoping. Yet I would 
not claim to be uncertain about the existence of Deng. I am simply *less* 
certain about Deng than I am about noncontradiction.

In PCR terms, if I faced a criticism that brought into question either my 
belief in the existence of Deng or my belief in noncontradiction, I would 
have to respond to that criticism by abandoning my belief in the 
existence of Deng, because I am *less* certain of that belief than I am 
of noncontradiction. And yet I must insist that to call my current belief 
in the existence of Deng "uncertainty" would be abuse of language.

I hope I didn't drag that out too long, but I had a hard time following 
it myself. Hence the redundancy.

