  .... large amount of stuff elided...

Yes, but could you answer the question? Can you think of no two *specific*
beliefs that you are both certain about , but one of which you are much
*more* certain than the other. I expect that you can, and this is all I
meant when I claimed that certainty is relative. Nothing more and nothing
less. Your long reply seems to no longer dispute this small claim of mine,
but it was hard for me to tell. 

> The criticism may introduce doubt until you have rearranged this whole package
> to adjust to the criticism, but then you are certain again.  Being in a state of
> doubt is debilitating, it saps energy (will-power) and is most often a condition
> reflecting internal cross-purposes or ignorance of the epistemological
> boundaries of consensual reality.

But when you rearrange the package, which "certain" beliefs get booted? 
The one of which you are least certain, or course. That they got booted 
itself constitutes evidence that you were less certain of them than you 
were of some of the ideas which did *not* get booted.

And I still contend that doubt is not debilitating in and of itself. 
Doubt about certain important matters (such as whether or not one will 
die before dawn) can be debilitating, but doubt in general is not.

Relativism does not necessarily mean, as you seem to fear it must, that 
everything is equally true, that we cannot distinguish between good ideas 
and bad ones. It does not necessarily mean that we cannot decide or that 
we cannot act. Just because absolute certainty is impossible doesn't mean 
we have to consign ourselves to living the lives of villains in an Ayn 
Rand novel. To believe so is just as bad (though opposite to) "leftist 
nihilism".

